  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 214 PM CST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nWidely variable conditions are being observed across the CWA this \nafternoon as a cold front is sweeping through. Relatively speaking, \nit was a very warm start to the day as 7 AM observed temps were in \nthe low 50s to low 60s. Temperatures have fallen through the morning \nas cold air advection occurs behind the cold front, especially in \nnorth-central MN where air temps as of early this afternoon are\naround 10 degrees cooler in the low 40s. Expect CAA to continue\ninto tonight, with forecast low temps tonight in the mid 20s in\nnorth- central MN and in the mid 30s in northwest WI. \n\nIn addition to falling temperatures, a line of rain showers have \nbeen ongoing in northwest WI this afternoon ahead of the cold front. \nThis line of rain will continue to move east this afternoon into the \nevening as the cold front advances farther through the CWA. A surge \nof moisture advection this evening is very likely (60-85% chance) to \nkeep rain over large portions of northwest WI through tonight. \n\nAttention turns towards the potential for light snow as a 500mb \nshortwave trough propagates through the Upper Midwest on Sunday. As \n850mb temps drop to around -6 degC tonight into Sunday morning, lake \neffect snow is likely (65% chance) to develop in the lee of Lake of \nthe Woods and Rainy Lake in far northern MN and Lake Superior in \nnorth-central WI. Snow accumulations downwind of these lakes are \nexpected to be localized. Within these localized areas, amounts in \nexcess of 1\" are certainly possible with the NBM probability of \nexceedence for 1\" or greater showing a 40-60% chance. Higher amounts \nabove 4\" are unlikely as there is only a 15% chance for these higher \namounts. There are similar probabilities and expected snowfall \namounts for the snowbelt regions of northern Iron County around the \nPenokee Range.  \n\nOutside of lake effect snow, there is a 50% or greater chance for at \nleast 0.1\" of snow across the MN Arrowhead along and north of the \nIron Range on Sunday through Sunday night as this shortwave \npropagates through the CWA. Hi-res model soundings show the \npotential for steep low-level lapse rates and modest MUCAPE up to 50 \nJ/kg, which could lead to brief and localized heavier snowfall \nrates. The main impact from these heavier snow showers is expected \nto be rapidly reduced visibility. While visibility may be briefly \nlower, true snow squall impacts with snow covered roads are unlikely \non Sunday afternoon as MnDOT RWIS sites currently report road sfc \ntemps in the mid 40s and temps are not forecast to consistently drop \nbelow freezing for a prolonged period until Sunday evening. By \nSunday evening, instability decreases which will limit the potential \nfor any snow squalls. However, this will something to closely \nmonitor for tomorrow given the potential impacts to travel.  \n  \nAfter lake effect snow comes to an end on Monday morning in Iron \nCounty, precipitation chances will be minimal to none across the CWA \nthrough the upcoming workweek. Sub-freezing temps stick around for \nmost early in the week on Monday through Tuesday night. Starting \nWednesday into next weekend, a diurnal pattern develops with high \ntemps in the 30s to low 40s and overnight low temps in the teens to \nmid 20s.",
